From 8f06b5cdd4ba9067eab37606c9760e0e74f814e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 21:49:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/56] nouveau/vk: add the Horizon kernel-mode-driver backend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

NVK's kernel-mode-driver abstraction has one backend, and it is libdrm
talking to the nouveau kernel driver. This adds a second, for a platform
that has no DRM at all: Horizon OS, where the GPU is reached through the
`nv` system services.

The backend sits on horizon_gpu, a Vulkan-free GPU layer over those
services, and is named through -Dhorizon-gpu-dir because it lives
outside Mesa. There is no simulated render node, no synthetic file
descriptor and no re-implementation of the nouveau uAPI anywhere in it.

Enumeration takes the other hook the Vulkan runtime already offers.
try_create_for_drm walks drmGetDevices2, which needs a DRM subsystem to
walk; physical_devices.enumerate hands the whole job to the driver, and
that is what a platform with one SoC GPU wants. nvk_create_physical_device
is split out of nvk_create_drm_physical_device so both paths share
everything that is not about *how* the device was found.

Decision D9, which the hardware makes rather than the design: Horizon's
`nv` session is per process, and the GM20B characteristics can only be
queried once it is up — there is no way to describe the device without
opening it, which is what the nouveau backend does by opening the render
node a second time. So the pdev owns the horizon_gpu_device and every
nvkmd_dev created from it shares that one under a reference count. They
share the GPU address space, which is harmless: every VA in it is handed
out by horizon_gpu_vm_reserve and no two reservations overlap.

Decision D10: the parts of nv_device_info that are pure functions of the
chipset — shader model, warps per MP, MPs per TPC, the legal
shared-memory splits — move to src/nouveau/headers/nv_device_info_chipset.c,
next to the struct they fill. They were inside the DRM winsys, where a
second backend could not reach them and would have had to carry a copy
that drifts. Nothing about them changed.

Four smaller things had to give way, none of them Horizon-specific:

  vk_image::drm_format_mod was declared under #if DETECT_OS_LINUX ||
  DETECT_OS_BSD, so every driver reading it had to know which operating
  system it was on. The field is now unconditional and simply stays
  DRM_FORMAT_MOD_INVALID where the extension is not advertised; the
  entry point that implements VK_EXT_drm_format_modifier stays guarded,
  because that is a real platform dependency.

  nvk_descriptor_table.c and nvk_device_memory.c included <sys/mman.h>
  and used nothing from it.

  nvk_physical_device.c's major()/minor() decompose a DRM device number,
  and sys/sysmacros.h is glibc's home for them.

  nvk_instance.c took its driver identity from the ELF build-id note,
  which util/build_id.h only declares where dl_iterate_phdr exists. It
  now falls back to hashing PACKAGE_VERSION and MESA_GIT_SHA1. That is
  coarser — two builds of the same source with different local patches
  hash the same — and is recorded as a limitation rather than papered
  over with __DATE__, which would change the UUID on every rebuild.

Milestone items 1 and 2 (device, and GM20B info to nvkmd_pdev_info) are
implemented. Items 3 to 10 — memory objects, the VA heap, bind/unbind,
queue and channel, submit, fences, on-demand waits and destruction —
return VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT with the item number in the message,
so a caller reaching one gets a named gap rather than a wrong answer.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 items 1-2
Why: nouveau_ws is libdrm talking to the nouveau kernel driver. It is
  not a gap to patch on Horizon; it is the thing being replaced.
Evidence: `meson compile` for aarch64/horizon with
  -Dvulkan-drivers=nouveau now builds libnvk.a (94 162 bytes) with
  vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr and nvk_CreateInstance defined and the three
  backend objects in the archive. It previously stopped at
    FAILED: src/nouveau/winsys/libnouveau_ws.a.p/nouveau_bo.c.o
    nouveau_bo.c: fatal error: sys/mman.h: No such file or directory
Upstream: the four fixes above and the nv_device_info move, yes. The
  backend itself is new code for a platform Mesa does not yet target.

Absorbs (2026-08-23 series compaction; full history in mesa-patches/'s
own git log):

- 0025 "say why enumeration found no GPU": both empty-list paths of
  enumeration now log their cause, and the common one names
  NVK_I_WANT_A_BROKEN_VULKAN_DRIVER — nvk_is_conformant() refuses
  NV_DEVICE_TYPE_SOC and under NDEBUG that refusal printed nothing.
  Evidence: first hardware run of t_vulkan failed at
  "vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices -> 0" with no diagnostic anywhere, which
  cost a console round trip.

- 0033 "nonCoherentAtomSize is a CPU cache property": nc_atom_size_B is
  the cache write-back granule (cache_ops.h:65), not the services'
  compression page. GM20B reports a 0x20000 compression page and
  nvk_device_memory.c rounds flush ranges up to nc_atom_size_B, so a
  4 KiB buffer flushed with VK_WHOLE_SIZE ran `dc civac` across 128 KiB.
  Evidence: t_init on console reports "compression_page=0x20000"
  (2026-07-27 logs).
